{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 41, :comments ["<b>And he brought him up to Bamoth Baal, and from there he saw the edge of the people</b> – He would bring him up to a place where he would show it to him, so that he could direct himself toward it in his cursing and his soul would not be detached from them, for these are among the powers of the soul, to cleave at the time of sight, as is known from the matter of the Sages (Bava Kamma 117a): \"Pull me my cheese that I wish to see him — pull it to him; he set his eyes upon it and his soul departed.\" And Scripture told that he did not see the entire camp, for they were encamped as four standards toward the four winds of the heavens. And the second time Balak said to him: \"Only the edge of it you shall see, and all of it you shall not see\" (Numbers 23:13) — to say, even this time you shall not see it all, if that is the matter preventing you from cursing it; but \"curse it for me from there\" if you can, for I have no place to show you all of it from there. Balak thought that perhaps in one of the edges there is a standard of righteous and good men, and HaShem does not desire to crush them. And it is possible to interpret further, that the first time he saw it all, for Bamoth Baal is a high place, and they both saw from there the edge of the people, and they built the altars there, and Balaam walked to the top of the hill that was on that mountain and saw it all. And this is the meaning of \"וַיֵּלֶךְ שְׁפִי\" (Numbers 23:3) — that he walked to the שְׁפִי (bare hilltop) that was on it, according to the words of R. Abraham Ibn Ezra."], :book :numbers, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 22, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T04:08:03.226120615Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}